"""Authored study framing, not copied official statements or qualification claims.

Each tuple is (paraphrased task, reasoning invariant, first hint, important edge).
Existing cohort contracts/designs retain their exact declared source provenance.
"""
LESSONS = {
243: ('Find the minimum index distance between occurrences of two distinct target words.', 'After each word, the latest index of each target is sufficient to improve the best cross-target distance.', 'Ask whether an older occurrence can beat a newer occurrence for a future opposite target.', 'Repeated words must update their own latest index without losing the best earlier answer.'),
245: ('Find the minimum occurrence distance between two target words, including when both targets are the same.', 'Different targets use two latest positions; identical targets compare successive distinct occurrences.', 'Separate the equal-target case before designing the scan.', 'An occurrence cannot be paired with itself when both target words are equal.'),
246: ('Decide whether rotating a decimal string by 180 degrees reproduces the same string.', 'Every mirrored pair must match the rotation map; the middle character must rotate to itself.', 'Write the valid rotated digit pairs before scanning.', 'A middle 6 or 9 is invalid even though those digits form a valid mirrored pair.'),
247: ('Generate length-n decimal strings that are unchanged by 180-degree rotation, excluding multi-digit leading zeros.', 'A valid interior stays valid when surrounded by a compatible rotated digit pair.', 'Construct from the center instead of testing every length-n decimal string.', 'Zero is allowed as an interior pair but not as the outermost pair of a multi-digit result.'),
248: ('Count strobogrammatic decimal numbers within inclusive string bounds.', 'Generation by length avoids numeric overflow; only equal-length candidates need lexical boundary comparisons.', 'Reuse center-out generation and handle short and long endpoint lengths separately.', 'Count the single digit zero correctly without admitting padded multi-digit numbers.'),
249: ('Group strings that differ only by a uniform cyclic alphabet shift.', 'Two strings belong together exactly when their relative modulo-26 character differences agree.', 'Look for a signature unchanged by adding the same offset to every letter.', 'Wraparound shifts such as az and ba must share a signature; singleton strings need a consistent empty signature.'),
250: ('Count binary-tree subtrees whose nodes all have the same value.', 'A subtree is uniform only if both child subtrees are uniform and each child root agrees with its parent.', 'Have postorder traversal return a fact that its parent can combine.', 'Both child calls must run even if the first is nonuniform, because uniform descendants still contribute.'),
254: ('Enumerate nondecreasing factorizations of an integer using factors greater than one.', 'A minimum next factor prevents duplicate orderings while the remaining quotient preserves the required product.', 'Choose the next divisor and recurse only on the quotient.', 'The trivial factorization containing only the original number is excluded; prime inputs yield no combinations.'),
255: ('Determine whether distinct values can be a binary search tree preorder traversal.', 'The stack holds unfinished ancestors; entering a right subtree raises a permanent lower bound.', 'Distinguish descending into a left child from climbing to an ancestor’s right child.', 'A later value below the established lower bound invalidates the traversal even when local neighbors look plausible.'),
256: ('Minimize painting cost for a row of houses using three colors, with adjacent houses different.', 'Each color state is the cheapest valid prefix ending in that color.', 'The next state needs only the other two previous color costs.', 'Update a new row of states so one house’s newly computed costs never contaminate its other colors.'),
259: ('Count index triples whose values sum to less than a target.', 'After sorting, a successful left/right pair makes every smaller right endpoint valid for the same first and left indices.', 'Use sorting to count a whole range of triples at once.', 'Duplicate values still represent different indices, and sorting must operate on a copy for this wrapper.'),
265: ('Minimize adjacent-different painting cost with an arbitrary number of colors.', 'The previous row’s smallest and second-smallest costs answer every exclusion query for the current color.', 'Avoid rescanning all previous colors for every current color.', 'Equal minimum costs need distinct color identities; excluding one minimum must not exclude another equal minimum.'),
267: ('Generate all distinct palindromic permutations of a string.', 'At most one character count is odd; each unique half permutation determines exactly one mirrored palindrome.', 'Count characters before permuting anything.', 'Repeated half characters must not produce duplicate permutations, and the odd center is not part of the half search.'),
269: ('Infer a character ordering consistent with a dictionary sorted in an unknown alphabet.', 'The first differing characters in adjacent words create precedence edges; a valid output is a topological ordering.', 'Only one position in an adjacent word pair can establish new ordering information.', 'A longer word immediately preceding its own prefix is invalid even if the character graph has no cycle.'),
270: ('Find the binary search tree value closest to a numeric target.', 'BST ordering lets the search discard the opposite branch while retaining the best value encountered.', 'Compare the target with the current node to choose one next branch.', 'The cohort chooses the smaller value on equal distance; keep that tie rule explicit rather than relying on traversal order.'),
272: ('Select k binary search tree values closest to a numeric target.', 'Inorder values are sorted, so a bounded moving window can discard a farther endpoint as new values arrive.', 'Use sorted order to reason about which candidate can be removed safely.', 'Membership and output ordering are separate requirements; the cohort’s deterministic order is not a general uniqueness proof.'),
276: ('Count fence colorings where no three consecutive posts share a color.', 'Track prefixes whose final two colors match separately from prefixes whose final two colors differ.', 'Ask which previous state permits repeating the last color.', 'Small post counts and a single available color need deliberate base cases.'),
296: ('Choose a meeting location minimizing total Manhattan distance from occupied grid cells.', 'Manhattan distance separates into two independent one-dimensional median problems.', 'Solve row distance and column distance independently.', 'Many people may share a row or column; their multiplicity must influence the median and total distance.'),
298: ('Find the longest downward parent-to-child path whose values increase by exactly one.', 'The running chain extends only when the child is exactly its parent plus one; otherwise it restarts.', 'A consecutive sequence is a path property, not a sorted set of values.', 'A valid chain may begin below the root, and equal or decreasing values break it.'),
302: ('Find the area of the smallest axis-aligned rectangle containing the black pixels.', 'The extrema of black row and column indices determine the enclosing rectangle.', 'Track four boundaries instead of storing every black coordinate.', 'Coordinate differences need inclusive widths and heights; a single pixel has area one.'),
311: ('Multiply two compatible integer matrices while exploiting zero entries.', 'Each nonzero A entry contributes its value times the corresponding B row to one result row.', 'Reorder multiplication loops so zero entries skip useful amounts of work.', 'Skipping zeros is an optimization, not a different multiplication rule; signed values and rectangular dimensions still matter.'),
314: ('List binary-tree nodes by vertical column, preserving top-to-bottom order and appropriate same-level traversal order.', 'Breadth-first traversal visits shallower nodes first while column offsets identify their output bucket.', 'Carry both a node and its column in the queue.', 'Depth-first collection can incorrectly order nodes that share a column but have different depths.'),
317: ('Find an empty grid cell with minimum total traversable distance to every building.', 'Each empty cell accumulates distance and a count of buildings that reached it.', 'Run a traversal from each building and combine results only after all traversals.', 'A low-distance cell is unusable if even one building cannot reach it.'),
320: ('Generate all generalized abbreviations by replacing consecutive omitted characters with their run length.', 'An omission run is emitted once when it ends, rather than as several adjacent numeric fragments.', 'Treat each character as kept or omitted, and defer counting output until a run closes.', 'Flush a trailing omission count and ensure consecutive omitted characters become one number.'),
325: ('Find the longest contiguous subarray whose sum is k, allowing negative values.', 'For a prefix sum s, the earliest occurrence of s-k gives the longest valid subarray ending here.', 'Store the first position for each prefix sum rather than the latest.', 'The zero prefix before the array is needed to detect valid subarrays beginning at index zero.'),
333: ('Find the largest subtree that is itself a binary search tree.', 'A parent needs validity, minimum, maximum and size from each child to validate the combined subtree.', 'Return a compact postorder summary instead of checking every subtree independently.', 'A valid descendant subtree still counts when its ancestor violates BST ordering.'),
340: ('Find the longest substring containing at most k distinct characters.', 'The maintained window is valid exactly when its frequency map has at most k positive-count keys.', 'Expand right, then shrink left until the distinct-count rule is restored.', 'Delete zero-count keys and handle k equal to zero without leaving a stale nonempty window.'),
351: ('Count Android unlock patterns within a given length range, respecting intermediate-key visitation rules.', 'A move is legal when its target is unused and any key directly crossed has already been visited.', 'Represent the special crossed-key moves explicitly before backtracking.', 'A previously visited intermediate key permits a jump that would otherwise be illegal.'),
356: ('Determine whether a point set is symmetric about some vertical line.', 'The reflection candidate has doubled x-coordinate minX+maxX, so each point requires its mirrored partner.', 'Avoid floating-point line coordinates by working with twice the reflection position.', 'Points lying on the reflection line map to themselves; preserve the cohort’s treatment of duplicate points.'),
358: ('Rearrange a string so equal characters are at least k positions apart, or report impossibility.', 'Only characters whose cooldown has expired may enter the next-position priority queue.', 'Separate the best available character from characters temporarily unavailable due to recent use.', 'Remaining characters plus an empty available heap means failure; cooldown eligibility depends on output position.'),
360: ('Apply a quadratic function to a sorted array and return the transformed values in sorted order.', 'A convex quadratic places the largest remaining result at an endpoint; concavity reverses the output direction.', 'Compare transformed endpoints rather than sorting all transformed values.', 'The sign of the quadratic coefficient changes which end of the result should be filled first.'),
361: ('Place one bomb in an empty cell to maximize enemies hit along rows and columns until walls stop the blast.', 'Every wall-separated row or column segment shares one reusable enemy count.', 'Compute a segment count only when entering it after a wall or grid boundary.', 'Walls reset visibility; enemies beyond a wall must never contribute to a candidate cell.'),
366: ('Group tree nodes by the round in which repeated leaf removal would remove them.', 'A node’s removal round is one plus the maximum removal round of its children.', 'Compute height from leaves using postorder traversal.', 'Removal rounds can be computed without repeatedly mutating or rescanning the whole tree.'),
369: ('Add one to the decimal number stored by a linked list of digits.', 'The rightmost digit below nine absorbs the carry, and every later digit becomes zero.', 'A sentinel handles the case where every input digit is nine.', 'Native linked-node mutation and the nonmutating digit-array wrapper have different ownership behavior.'),
370: ('Apply inclusive range increments to an initially zero array.', 'An update adds its delta at the left boundary and cancels it immediately after the right boundary.', 'Record how values change at boundaries, then reconstruct by prefix sums.', 'Do not write a cancellation beyond the array; overlapping and negative updates must combine correctly.'),
156: ('Rotate a constrained binary tree upside down along its left spine.', 'At each spine step preserve the next left child and old right child before rewiring pointers.', 'Draw one rotation with three nodes before writing the pointer assignments.', 'Overwriting a pointer before saving its old value loses a subtree; native node ownership differs from JSON fixtures.'),
159: ('Find the longest substring containing at most two distinct characters.', 'After shrinking, the active frequency map represents exactly the characters of the current valid window.', 'Keep a sliding window and remove characters from its left when a third distinct one appears.', 'Zero-count entries must disappear from the map so its key count remains meaningful.'),
161: ('Decide whether one insertion, deletion or replacement transforms one string into the other.', 'After the first mismatch, exactly one allowed alignment shift must make the remaining suffixes agree.', 'Handle length difference before comparing the first mismatched characters.', 'Equal strings are zero edits, not one; a lone trailing character may be the only edit.'),
163: ('List missing inclusive ranges inside bounds for a sorted integer array.', 'The next missing candidate moves beyond every observed value without enumerating the missing integers.', 'Use the gap between consecutive observed values and the endpoint bounds.', 'A missing singleton and an empty input both need the same gap logic as longer ranges.'),
280: ('Reorder values into alternating non-strict valley and peak inequalities.', 'After processing a neighbor pair, earlier alternating inequalities remain satisfied.', 'An adjacent swap can repair the current inequality without sorting the whole array.', 'The cohort currently exposes a copied-array JSON result; separately confirm any required native in-place behavior.'),
286: ('Fill empty-room cells with their shortest grid distance to a gate, keeping walls and gates fixed.', 'Breadth-first expansion from all gates assigns each first-visited room its minimum distance.', 'Put every gate in the initial queue rather than traversing separately from each room.', 'Unreachable rooms retain the infinity marker; separately confirm the native mutation contract.'),
294: ('Decide whether a player has a winning strategy when a move changes two adjacent plus signs into minus signs.', 'Independent plus runs are impartial subgames, and their Grundy values combine by XOR.', 'First solve the possible splits of a single plus run.', 'The number of legal immediate moves alone does not determine whether the entire position is winning.'),
737: ('Check word-by-word sentence similarity when supplied similar-word pairs are symmetric and transitive.', 'Union-find components represent the transitive closure of the supplied equivalence relationships.', 'Treat the pair list as connectivity rather than as a direct lookup table.', 'Identical words are similar without appearing in any pair; different sentence lengths fail immediately.'),
742: ('Find a leaf nearest to a target node in a binary tree.', 'Treating parent links as additional edges lets breadth-first search explore every shortest route from the target.', 'The nearest leaf can lie outside the target’s own descendant subtree.', 'Leaf status comes from the original tree’s children, not from degree alone in the undirected graph.'),
751: ('Cover a consecutive range of IPv4 addresses with a minimum sequence of CIDR blocks.', 'Each chosen block starts at the current address, respects alignment and fits the number of addresses remaining.', 'Choose the largest aligned power-of-two block that does not overshoot.', 'Address zero needs special alignment handling, and unsigned arithmetic must not wrap at the IPv4 limit.'),
755: ('Simulate water droplets falling on a height profile using the specified left-first settling rule.', 'Each droplet settles at the first reachable lower level on the left, otherwise on the right, otherwise at the source.', 'Separate a reachable plateau from a strictly lower settling position.', 'Update the chosen height before processing the next droplet; left-side preference is observable.'),
758: ('Wrap covered substrings in bold tags, merging overlapping or adjacent covered regions.', 'A coverage mask combines all word matches before any output tags are emitted.', 'Mark matching character ranges first, then generate tags when coverage changes.', 'Overlapping matches must not create nested tags, and adjacent matches should share one bold segment.'),
772: ('Evaluate an arithmetic expression containing parentheses and the basic binary operators.', 'Operator precedence and parenthesis nesting determine which pending operation may be reduced next.', 'Separate expression, multiplicative term and atomic factor parsing.', 'Integer division truncates toward zero, which differs from floor division for negative intermediate values.'),
774: ('Add at most k gas stations to minimize the largest remaining adjacent-station gap.', 'For a proposed maximum gap, the required insertion count is monotone as the allowed gap grows.', 'Binary-search the answer instead of deciding every new station’s exact position.', 'Exact divisibility at a gap boundary affects the required count; floating-point acceptance needs an explicit policy.'),
903: ('Count permutations whose adjacent comparisons follow an increasing/decreasing pattern.', 'Rank-based DP summarizes possible last elements without remembering every partial permutation.', 'The next inequality asks for a prefix or suffix sum over previous ranks.', 'Ranks shift as a new element is inserted; use a fresh DP row and the required modulus.'),
911: ('Answer who led an election at past query times, with the most recent vote breaking ties.', 'The recorded leader changes whenever the current voter reaches or exceeds the previous maximum count.', 'Precompute leaders once, then locate the last vote no later than the query.', 'Ties prefer the current voter, and binary search must include votes cast exactly at the query time.'),
1213: ('Return values common to three strictly increasing integer arrays.', 'Advancing pointers whose values are below the current maximum cannot skip a common value.', 'Coordinate three monotone scans rather than comparing every combination.', 'Advance all pointers after finding a match so an output value is emitted only once.'),
1214: ('Determine whether one value from each of two binary search trees sums to a target.', 'A set of values from the first tree turns each second-tree value into a complement lookup.', 'The pair must use one node from each tree.', 'The same numeric value may appear in both trees; it is still a valid cross-tree pair.'),
1215: ('Enumerate numbers in an inclusive range whose adjacent decimal digits differ by exactly one.', 'Appending lastDigit-1 or lastDigit+1 preserves the stepping-number property.', 'Generate valid prefixes directly instead of examining every integer in the range.', 'Zero is a valid standalone number, but leading zeros must not create duplicate representations.'),
1229: ('Find the earliest overlap of two availability schedules that lasts at least a required duration.', 'For the current interval pair, the earlier-ending interval cannot help after that endpoint.', 'Sort valid intervals, compare overlap starts and ends, and advance one pointer.', 'An overlap exactly equal to the requested duration is valid; do not discard it with a strict comparison.'),
1230: ('Compute the probability of obtaining exactly a target number of heads from biased coin tosses.', 'After each coin, each head-count state combines its prior tails path and preceding heads path.', 'Use a probability distribution over head count rather than enumerate all toss outcomes.', 'Update counts in the correct direction or with a fresh row so one coin is not used repeatedly.'),
1692: ('Count ways to partition distinct candies into a fixed number of nonempty unlabeled groups, modulo the required constant.', 'The newest candy either starts a new group or joins one of the existing groups.', 'Derive the recurrence from the placement of the final candy.', 'Groups are unlabeled: multiplying by arbitrary group-name permutations would overcount.'),
1714: ('Answer sums of array positions reached by repeatedly adding a query-specific step.', 'Queries with a small shared step can reuse suffix sums; large steps visit only a few positions.', 'Split queries by step size around the square root of the array length.', 'The preprocessing start index and modular arithmetic must agree with the direct-scan query path.'),
1740: ('Find the edge distance between two values in a binary tree.', 'The lowest common ancestor divides the desired path into two downward paths.', 'Locate the common ancestor, then measure each target’s depth relative to it.', 'When both target values are identical the distance is zero, not the depth of that node.'),
1820: ('Maximize accepted invitations when each person and seat can participate in at most one allowed pair.', 'An augmenting path may move earlier matches to free a seat for the next person.', 'A failed direct assignment can still succeed by rearranging existing matches.', 'Visited-seat marks belong to one augmentation attempt; keeping them forever prevents valid later rearrangements.'),
1826: ('Identify which sensor stream is consistent with dropping one reading and shifting the following readings.', 'Only suffix alignment after the first disagreement can distinguish the two candidate faulty streams.', 'Skip one position in each stream as separate hypotheses.', 'Repeated readings can leave both hypotheses possible; preserve the required ambiguous-result behavior.'),
1908: ('Decide whether the player to move can win normal-play Nim.', 'A zero XOR of pile sizes is losing under optimal play; a nonzero XOR admits a move to zero.', 'Look for a bit position where the aggregate parity is nonzero.', 'The theorem assumes the stated normal-play rule; changing the final-move winner changes the game.'),
1918: ('Find the kth smallest sum among contiguous subarrays of a positive-valued array.', 'For a proposed sum bound, a sliding window counts how many subarrays fit it.', 'Binary-search a sum threshold and count candidates without materializing all sums.', 'The counting window relies on positivity; negative values invalidate the monotone shrinking argument.'),
1924: ('Find a smallest circle enclosing all supplied points.', 'When a point lies outside the maintained circle, it must constrain a replacement boundary.', 'Start with one point and rebuild using one, two or three boundary points as needed.', 'Deterministic input order has cubic worst-case work; no expected-linear claim follows without a distribution or shuffle.'),
1933: ('Check whether a digit string can be partitioned into equal-character triples and exactly one equal-character pair.', 'Each maximal equal-character run contributes a remainder modulo three.', 'Track how many runs need a pair rather than generating every partition.', 'A remainder of one is impossible, and exactly one remainder-two contribution is required globally.'),
1940: ('Return the common subsequence shared by strictly increasing arrays.', 'Within a strictly increasing array, every value appears once, so a value present in every array belongs to the answer.', 'Use uniqueness within each array to simplify frequency counting.', 'This shortcut depends on strict increase; arbitrary arrays require a genuine order-sensitive subsequence algorithm.')
}
